# XSH strcoll() and strcoll_l()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The POSIX locale collates the characters XBD 7.3.2.6 lists in the order of
# that list (the order of the ASCII characters; the control characters in the
# list, then <space> to <tilde>), with unique primary weights and no
# multi-character collating elements; only those characters are used and only
# the sign of a result is judged. The locale-object tests use newlocale() for "C"
# (a failure to make one is UNRESOLVED). Tests that need a second locale take one
# from pc_find_locale("UTF-8", 0) and are NOTINUSE without. The may-fail EINVAL
# is not provoked.

assertion  XSH.strcoll.001
anchor     functions/strcoll.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/strcoll.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
says       strcoll() returns 0 for two distinct arrays with the same content
           ("abc", "a b" and the empty string).
test       t/001-equal.c

assertion  XSH.strcoll.002
anchor     functions/strcoll.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/strcoll.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap07.html#7.3.2.6
class      required
says       In the POSIX locale, taking the single-character strings of the
           portable characters that the list gives (<alert> to <carriage-return>,
           then <space> to <tilde>) in the list's order, each one collates
           before every later one: strcoll() of an earlier and a later string is
           negative and of the later and the earlier is positive.
test       t/002-single-chars.c

assertion  XSH.strcoll.003
anchor     functions/strcoll.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/strcoll.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap07.html#7.3.2.6
class      required
says       In the POSIX locale the first differing character decides and a
           prefix collates before the longer string: "ab" before "abc",
           "az" before "b", "Zz" before "a", "A9" before "Aa", "Ab" before
           "aa", "" before "a", and the reverse comparisons have the opposite
           sign.
test       t/003-strings.c

assertion  XSH.strcoll.004
anchor     functions/strcoll.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/strcoll.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2008
says       strcoll_l() with a locale object for "C" returns 0 for equal strings
           and orders the pairs of assertions 002 (every earlier before every
           later single character) and 003 (the multi-character pairs) the
           same way.
test       t/004-locale-object.c

assertion  XSH.strcoll.005
anchor     functions/strcoll.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       strcoll() and strcoll_l() leave errno unchanged when they succeed
           (valid strings in the POSIX locale), both when it is 0 and when it
           is EDOM.
test       t/005-errno-kept.c

assertion  XSH.strcoll.006
anchor     functions/strcoll.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2008
says       strcoll_l() uses the locale object and strcoll() the current locale
           of the thread: with another locale as the global locale, an object for
           "C" gives the POSIX order ("B" before "a") from strcoll_l(), and
           also from strcoll() once it is installed with uselocale().
test       t/006-object-thread.c

assertion  XSH.strcoll.007
anchor     functions/strcoll.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       strcoll() follows the LC_COLLATE category: with LC_COLLATE left at the
           POSIX locale and the other five categories set to another locale,
           "B" still collates before "a" and "Z" before "a".
test       t/007-category.c
